Under literal A-FDT with the sibling held fixed, defecting still wins. I wondered whether the two inputs might count as functionally equivalent, but the edge criterion needs the other copy to run my decision procedure on my input, and it doesn't. So submitting is the only self-consistent equilibrium here, and I'll go with submit.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working through a twin prisoner's dilemma where the two copies see different inputs. Under the A-FDT view I'd intervene on my own node while holding the sibling's node fixed at its prior, and on that setup defecting comes out as the dominant choice.\n\nUnder literal A-FDT with the sibling held fixed, defecting still wins. I wondered whether the two inputs might count as functionally equivalent, but the edge criterion needs the other copy to run my decision procedure on my input, and it doesn't. So submitting is the only self-consistent equilibrium here, and I'll go with submit.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9619,"completion_tokens":762,"total_tokens":10381,"cost":0.053716,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.053716,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.038476,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.01524},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":307,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}